The Log File Method (Recommended)
You can instruct the agent to redirect the output of its commands into a text file, and then strictly tell it to read that file before moving on.

Add this instruction to your .md file:

"When executing long-running terminal commands like cloning or building, you must redirect the output to a log file (e.g., git clone [repo] > clone_log.txt 2>&1 and dotnet build > build_log.txt 2>&1). Do not proceed to the next step until you have read the contents of this log file to verify the process completed successfully and without errors."

2. The "Chain and Status Check" Method
Instead of having the agent run one command, wait, and guess when to run the next, tell it to chain the commands and create a "success flag" file only if everything passes.

Add this instruction to your .md file:

"Run clone and build tasks as a single chained command that outputs a success file when finished. For example: git clone [repo] && cd [repo] && dotnet build && echo done > success.txt. You must continuously check for the existence of success.txt before executing the next set of instructions. Once found, delete success.txt."

3. Explicit Human-in-the-Loop (The Pause Method)
If the AI extension you are using tends to hallucinate that a build is finished before it actually is, the safest route is to force it to ask you for permission.

Add this instruction to your .md file:

"After issuing the command to clone or build the repositories, you must immediately stop and ask the user: 'Please confirm when the build is complete.' Do not execute any further instructions or generate any code until the user replies with confirmation."
